L422: # **Time (days)**
L423: PROJECT DESCRIPTION - Antioxidant-rich diet and altitude training
L424: The effect of increased intake of antioxidant-rich foods on exercise adaptation, redox balance and
L425: inflammation among top athletes during a moderate altitude training session (2300m)
L426: Koivisto A [1], Siv Kjølsrud Bøhn [2], Ingvild Paur [2], Gøran Paulsen [1], Espen Tønnesen [1], Ina Garthe [1], Truls
L427: Raastad [3], Rune Blomhoff [2]
L428: 1Norwegian Olympic Sports Centre, Sognsveien 228, 0806 Oslo, 2Department of nutrition, Faculty of
L429: Medicine, Universitety of Oslo, [3] Dept. of Physical Performance, Norwegian School of Sport Sciences,
L430: Oslo, Norway
L431: **Summary**
L432: Athletes, especially practicipating in endurance sports, are at increased risk of oxidative stress and
L433: inflammation-related diseases and injuries. During exercise, and especially during exercise at altitude,
L434: the production of reactive oxygen and nitrogen compounds (RONS) and inflammation markers
L435: increases. Intake of antioxidant supplements has become a common nutritional strategy among athletes
L436: who believe that it is a plausible measure to prevent oxidative stress and oxidative damage. However, a
L437: transient increase of RONS is required to activate signal cascades that initiate training adaptation.
L438: Several independent studies have shown that antioxidant supplements hamper training adaptation,
L439: possibly by interfering with / reducing the signalling cascades initiated by RONS. However, it is not
L440: known whether a high intake of antioxidant-rich foods can affect the overproduction of RONS,
L441: inflammatory markers and / or exercise adaptation. We therefore want to investigate whether an
L442: increased intake of natural antioxidants in the form of fruits, vegetables and berries, in line with the
L443: official Norwegian dietary guidelines, can affect antioxidant status, immune defense and training
L444: adaptation in connection with altitude training in Norwegian elite athletes who are candidates for the
L445: 2016 Olympics Games.
L446: **Background**
L447: Reactive oxygen and nitrogen compounds (RONS) are continuously formed as a result of natural cell
L448: metabolism. The body's antioxidant defense can usually neutralize and disarm these compounds before
L449: the cell's lipids, proteins and DNA become damaged, but if there is an imbalance between RONS and the
L450: cell's antioxidant capacity, it will cause a state of oxidative stress. Many chronic lifestyle diseases are
L451: associated with oxidative stress (Hu 2003), and athletes, especially in endurance sports, are at increased
L452: risk of oxidative stress and inflammation-related diseases and injuries (Carlsen, Hem et al. 2011, Metz,
L453: Wustrack et al. 2012, Turagam, Velagapudi et al. 2012, Dean, Gettings et al. 2015, Elkington, Gleeson et
L454: al. 2015, Sugama, Suzuki et al. 2015). During exercise, production of RONS increases in a dosedependent manner (Knez, Jenkins et al. 2014). This can result in disrupted cell function and contribute to
L455: muscular fatigue and possibly delayed recovery (Powers and Jackson 2008). Antioxidants, a common
L456: term for molecules with the ability to protect other molecules from being oxidized, thus have the
L457: potential to counteract oxidative stress. Therefore, many athletes believe that it is necessary to
L458: consume large amounts of antioxidants in the form of supplements, to protect themselves from
L459: exercise-induced overproduction of RONS. Despite possible negative effects of oxidative stress, a
L460: transient increase in RONS is an important signal for exercise adaptation. RONS seems to activate
L461: multiple signal molecules, including PGC-1α, which is a chief regulator of mitochondrial biogenesis
L462: (Powers, Duarte et al. 2010). Previous studies have shown that large single dose antioxidant
L463: supplements can impair the adaptive response to training(Gomez-Cabrera, Domenech et al. 2008,
L464: Ristow, Zarse et al. 2009, Paulsen, Cumming et al. 2014). Therefore, it is speculated that antioxidant
L465: supplements prevent exercise-induced adaptation by interfering with the signal cascades initiated by
L466: RONS.
L467: It is not known whether a high intake of natural antioxidants via ingestion of fruits and vegetables can
L468: have similar undesirable effects on training adaptation. A high intake of fruits and vegetables, naturally
L469: rich in antioxidants, protects against a variety of diseases associated with oxidative stress. In addition, an
L470: antioxidant-poor diet, e.g. low intake of fruits and vegetables, among athletes has been associated with
L471: increased levels of systemic inflammatory markers (Watson, Callister et al. 2005, Plunkett, Callister et al.
L472: 2010). Since fruits and vegetables are rich in antioxidants, it has been suggested that food-based
L473: antioxidants provide additional protection against oxidation in the body. However, since fruits and
L474: vegetables contain tens of thousands of different plant chemicals (phytochemicals), it is not possible to
L475: conclude that the antioxidants are responsible for the disease protection. Most phytochemicals have
L476: antioxidant properties, but they also have other properties that can have different effects in the body.
L477: Among other things, other properties of the phytochemicals can be important for optimizing the body's
L478: own defense systems (Bøhn, Myhrstad et al. 2010).
L479: At the Norwegian Olympic Sports Centre, athletes are advised not to use high doses of antioxidant
L480: supplements. In general, it is recommended to increase the intake of antioxidant-rich foods during
L481: periods of high exercise load as a strategy to avoid negative effects of oxidative stress. Norwegian health
L482: authorities recommend at least five servings of fruit, berries and vegetables per day (500g)
L483: (Helsedirektoratet, 2014). During an altitude training session in June 2015 it was revealed that the intake
L484: of fruit and vegetables among elite athletes is far below the Heath Authorities recommendations.
L485: Furthermore, it is uncertain whether the recommended “five a day” provides a sufficient intake of
L486: antioxidants for endurance athletes during their most demanding training periods.
L487: In order to test whether natural antioxidants from plant foods have an effect on exercise adaptation, we
L488: want to perform a clinical controlled study on athletes exposed to extra high oxidative stress levels at
L489: moderate altitude (2300 m). Altitude exposure, which reduces oxygen availability (hypoxia), has
L490: previously been shown to increase the oxidative stress levels (Pialoux, Mounier et al. 2009, Pialoux,
L491: Mounier et al. 2009).
L492: Studies using the "live high - train low" model (training at 1200m and living at 2500-3000m simulated
L493: height) among elite athletes have shown increased oxidative stress. At the same time, the antioxidant
L494: capacity in serum has been reduced when the duration of altitude exposure has passed 18 days (Pialoux,
L495: Mounier et al. 2009, Pialoux, Mounier et al. 2009).
L496: Given that exposure to altitude and increased exercise load will increase oxidative stress levels, it is
L497: possible that athletes' redox balance can be changed during altitude training (the "live high-train high"
L498: method). Altitude training will also strain the body's immune system. Previous studies have shown that
L499: exercise in hypoxia increases secretion of some cytokines and leads to transient immunosuppression
L500: (Mazzeo 2005).
L501: Finally, it is unknown how an antioxidant-rich diet will affect exercise adaptation, antioxidant balance,
L502: immune defense and gene expression during altitude training. There is lack of data on the general
L503: antioxidant intake during altitude training in elite athletes. There is also a lack of reference values for
L504: biomarkers for antioxidant capacity and oxidative stress in elite athletes (Lewis, Howatson et al. 2015).
L505: ### Aims of the study
L506: The purpose of the study is to investigate whether an antioxidant-rich diet can impact the health-related
L507: effects of altitude training in elite athletes by optimizing snack foods in the intervention group for intake
L508: of antioxidant-rich foods during a 3-week training camp in moderate altitude (2300 m). Specifically, we
L509: want to explore the dietary intake of antioxidants, and oxidative stress and antioxidant capacity before,
L510: during and after the altitude training camp. Furthermore, we want to study the effects of an
L511: antioxidant-rich diet on redox balance, inflammation status and exercise adaptation during a 3-week
L512: altitude training camp. With a special focus on the stress response (the body's own defense systems) we
L513: will study how the altitude sojourn affects the gene expression profile and whether the intervention
L514: affects these defense systems compared to the controls.
L515: ### Specific hypotheses
L516: 1. Adaptation to altitude measured as increased hemoglobin mass will not be affected by increased
L517: intake of antioxidant rich meals.
L518: 2. Sports performance will have a more positive development during the altitude training camp
L519: following intake of antioxidant-rich foods, because muscles and the circulatory system will better
L520: withstand the combination of hypoxia and large exercise volume.
L521: 3. Athletes have suboptimal intake of fruits, berries and vegetables (<500g per day) in lowland and at
L522: altitude.
L523: 4. The altitude exposure combined with large exercise volume will increase oxidative stress and reduce
L524: antioxidant capacity. This will result in increased levels of inflammatory biomarkers.
L525: 5. Daily intake of antioxidant-rich foods will increase circulating levels of selected antioxidants and limit
L526: the increase in oxidative stress and inflammation biomarkers.
L527: 6. Altitude training results in changes in the gene expression profile in blood cells, especially genes
L528: involved in the stress response. Altitude-induced changes in gene expression profile are different
L529: between the groups.
L530: ### Outcome variables
L531: Effect of altitude training camp: e.g. hemoglobin mass and blood volume, maximum oxygen uptake
L532: (VO2max), lactate profile, performance in sport-specific tests.
L533: - Anthropometry: e.g. body weight, lean body mass (LBM), fat mass (FM), bone density (BMD)
L534: - Diet: e.g. energy, macronutrients, micronutrients, number of servings of fruits, vegetables and berries,
L535: as well as total antioxidant intake (mmol)
L536: - Compliance: biomarkers for fruit and vegetable intake (e.g. carotenoids)
L537: - Inflammation markers: e.g. IL-1, IL-6, TNF
L538: - Gene expression: Whole genomic gene expression
L539: - Antioxidant status:
L540:     - Oxidative stress biomarkers: e.g. lipid peroxidation (e.g. 8-isoPGF2α).
L541:     - Antioxidant capacity: e.g. ferric reducing ability of plasma (FRAP)
L542:   - Total oxidative stress: e.g. dROM
L543: - Antioxidants in plasma: e.g. carotenoids, flavonoids
L544: - Other data from routine medical screening of athletes (e.g., estradiol, testosterone, crp, cortisol, hb,
L545: ferritin, hematocrit, vitamin D, vitamin E, B12, folate, cholesterol, HDL, LDL)
L546: ## Participants
L547: ### Inclusion criteria
L548: National team athletes, men and women (18-42 years) from paddling, rowing, cycling, swimming and
L549: triathlon who will attend a height gathering for the 2016 Olympics. Current elite athletes in endurance
L550: sports will be asked to participate.
L551: ### Exclusion criteria
L552:   - Allergies to the relevant foods where it is not possible to replace foods with similar antioxidantrich products in consultation with the study participant.
L553:   - Use of medications that can affect exercise and performance tests.
L554:   - Injuries that may affect exercise and performance tests.
L555: ### Recruitment
L556: Participants will be recruited among endurance athletes that have the potential to participate in
L557: Olympic Games 2016. The athletes will be randomized to an intervention group or control group
L558: stratified for sport category and gender.
L559: ### Power Calculations
L560: Based on a power size of 1.0, with a two-sided significance level of 5% and 80% power, we need 17
L561: athletes in each group. Estimated change in effect size is based on the variation in performance tests in
L562: 2000 m rowing on the rowing-ergometer where the variation from day to day is 1.3% (SD) under
L563: otherwise equal conditions. A change in performance of 1.3% corresponds to approx. 5 second
L564: improvement of 2000 m rowing; an improvement that can separate medal winners in an Olympics. The
L565: same relative changes are expected in performance on the other sports represented in the experiment.
L566: If we calculate a dropout of 10%, we will need 19 in each group to detect significant differences
L567: between the groups. We want to invite all current practitioners to participate (n = 45) because we
L568: expect 85% to agree to participate.
L569: ### Timeline
L570: Participants will be recruited four weeks prior to the altitude training camp, at the end of September
L571: 2015. The intervention and altitude training camp in Spain will end on November 11, 2015. All
L572: participants will undergo a general medical examination and obtain clinical parameters three weeks
L573: ahead of altitude stay. In addition, hemoglobin mass and body composition are assessed one week
L574: before and after the altitude camp. Repeated records of diet, anthropometry and performance, as well
L575: as blood sampling, will be carried out before, during and after the altitude camp (Figure 1).
L576: Figure 1. Timeline of the study.
L577: ## Methods
L578: ### The intervention
L579: The purpose of the intervention is to optimize the in between-meals foods for athletes during the
L580: altitude training camp, specifically regarding the intake of antioxidant-rich foods. The intervention group
L581: will receive foods naturally high in antioxidants (including berries, nuts, dark chocolate, vegetables and
L582: fruits) (Carlsen, Halvorsen et al. 2010). The control group will receive foods that are usually consumed as
L583: in-between meals during the altitude camp. The calorie content of the snacks provided to the groups
L584: will be matched (~1000 kcal), and will be delivered once a day to each individual participant. The
L585: participants in both groups are otherwise encouraged to maintain stable body weight (avoid body
L586: weight abnormalities > 2%) throughout the entire altitude camp.
L587: ### The in-between meals in the intervention group (Daily)
L588: The aim is to double the antioxidant intake of the practitioners in the intervention group by offering a
L589: mix of antioxidant-rich snacks. The intervention described below is an example. Other common
L590: antioxidant-rich foods may also be included.
L591: - 3 pcs fruit / berry / vegetable smoothie selected from the following products (450 kcal):
L592:     - Bama, Turn 250 ml (71 kcal / 100ml): 44% grape juice, 30% banana puree, 17% pomegranate
L593: juice and 9% black currant juice
L594:     - Bama, Slide 250 ml (47 kcal / 100ml): 38% apple juice, 20% carrot puree, 20% mango puree
L595: 20%, 17% apple puree and 5% passion fruit puree
L596:     - Bama, Spin 250 ml (62 kcal / 100ml): 38% apple juice, 21% red grape juice, 20% banana puree,
L597: 8% blueberry puree, 8% black currant juice and 5% apple puree
L598: - 1 dl (40 g) walnuts (270 kcal)
L599: - 1 dl (50g) mixture of dried fruit and berries; cranberries, goji berries, apricots (Rema1000 and Bama)
L600: (140 kcal)
L601: - 40g dark chocolate (70%), selection of Freia Premium chocolates in several flavors; plain, pecan and
L602: sea salt, orange, almond, hazelnut (240kcal)
L603: ### The in-between meals in the control group (Daily)
L604: - 220 ml TINE milkshake in several flavors; strawberry and banana or cocoa (160 kcal)
L605: - 330ml TINE YT recovery drink in several flavors; strawberry and banana or cocoa (220 kcal)
L606: - 4 Bixit biscuits (60 g), or other type of sweet biscuits without fillings (290 kcal)
L607: - 1 dl (30g) Ritz salt biscuits / salt bars (120 kcal)
L608: - 50g Freia white chocolate (270 kcal)
L609: ### Antropometry
L610: Standardized DXA measurements (dual energy x-ray; iDXA; GE Healthcare) is taken before and after
L611: exercise camp to measure muscle mass (LBM), fat mass (FM) and bone density (BMD). Body weight with
L612: the least possible amount of clothing before breakfast is recorded daily and the practitioners are
L613: encouraged to maintain a stable body weight (avoid body weight abnormalities> 2%) throughout the
L614: altitude camp.
L615: ### Dietary recordings
L616: Four 24-hour recalls are conducted in an interview session before the altitude camp and three times
L617: during the altitude camp by the same registered dietitian. 24h recalls will be separated by at least 2 days
L618: in time. Interview guide for 24h recall follows in a separate attachment.
L619: ### Blood sampling
L620: Fasting blood samples with venipuncture are collected before and after elevation collection and on two
L621: test days during the altitude camp. In addition, weekly blood samples are taken from the fingertip
L622: during the height collection. Dried Blood Spot method is used which enables measurements of various
L623: biomarkers in a less invasive manner. The laboratory at UiO will analyze the blood samples with regard
L624: to biomarkers such as oxidative damage biomarkers, biomarkers of inflammatory status, antioxidant
L625: status and gene expression in blood cells. The protocol for blood sampling and handling is included in a
L626: separate appendix. Whole blood gene transcription assays, low density gene arrays and / or RT-PCR will
L627: be performed on blood cells collected at the various times.
L628: ### Bloodvolume
L629: Hemoglobin mass, plasma volume and total blood volume will be estimated by the CO-rebreathing
L630: method after Burge and Skinner (Burge and Skinner 1995). This procedure will be performed twice
L631: before and after the intervention period at altitude.
L632: ### Physiological tests and performance tests
L633: Participants in this study are all elite athletes with affiliation to the Norwegian Olympic Sports Center.
L634: Thus, all the athletes are experienced with the tests described below.
L635: The performance test and maximum oxygen uptake (VO2max) will only be tested at the Norwegian
L636: Olympic Sports Center (at sea-level) before and after the intervention period at altitude. The lactate
L637: profile test (without oxygen uptake measurements) will also be carried out at altitude: 4-5 days after the
L638: participants reach the altitude and 2-3 days before returning home.
L639: ### Lactate Profile Test
L640: Participants will perform a lactate profile test in a sports-specific ergometer / element (bicycle, rowing
L641: and paddle device, or water). This is carried out according to the standard procedure at Norwegian
L642: Olympic Sports Center (Stoa, Storen et al. 2010).
L643: After 10 minutes of heating at 60-70% of HF-max, 4 to 6 interval drafts are performed in 5 minutes with
L644: increasing intensity. The test ends when the blood lactate value is 1.5 mmol higher than the lactate
L645: value on the first two intervals. Between each interval draw there is a 30 second break. Lactate is
L646: measured in whole blood taken by fingerstick. The fingertip is washed and dried before each sample is
L647: taken. Oxygen uptake (see below) and heart rate are recorded along the way on each interval draw.
L648: ### VO2max
L649: In the extension of the lactate profile test, a VO2max test is carried out according to the standard
L650: procedure at Norwegian Olympic Sports Centre (Tonnessen, Haugen et al. 2015). The test starts at one
L651: intensity level under anaerobic threshold, and the intensity is increased every minute. The test is
L652: continued until exhaustion, and typically lasts for 4-6 minutes. Swimmers will measure VO2max with
L653: uphill running on a treadmill.
L654: ### Sport -specific test
L655: In a sports-specific ergometer / element, the participants will perform a distance test, where the goal is
L656: to produce as high average watt / speed as possible. The duration will vary from about 2 min to 15 min
L657: depending on the participant's sport.
L658: - Cycling: 10,000 m on a cycling roller
L659: - Rowing: 2000 m in Concept2 radar
L660: - Paddling: 1000 m Concept2 paddle device
L661: - Swimming: 200 m in water (own swimming species)
L662: Heart rate will be measured continuously while lactate will be measured 1 min after the test.
L663: ### Training diary records
L664: Before, during and after the altitude camp, the athletes log their training in the Norwegian Olympic
L665: Sports Centre’s online training diary. The training diary records the duration and intensity of the
L666: training, as well as the form of exercise and the form of movement. The training is documented in the
L667: same manner as previously described (Tonnessen, Sylta et al. 2014).
L668: ### Overview of the study protocol visits
L669: Visit 1: The measurements take place at the health department at Norwegian Olympic Sports Centre in
L670: Oslo.
L671:     - General medical examination
